.TH KFS 6
.SH NAME
kfs \- the on-disk format of kfs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B VKFS0001
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.IR kfs (4)
volume is four regions in a row: the superblock, a bitmap, an inode table,
and data.
Every number is little-endian and every region is a whole number of
4096-byte blocks, so a block is the unit of every read and write and
nothing straddles two.
.SS The superblock
Block 0.
.IP
.EX
+0   magic          "VKFS0001"
+8   blocksize      4096, and refused when it is not
+12  blocks         blocks in the whole volume
+16  bitmap_start   the bitmap's first block
+20  bitmap_blocks  how many blocks the bitmap takes
+24  inode_start    the inode table's first block
+28  inode_blocks   how many blocks the inode table takes
+32  data_start     the first block the bitmap hands out
+36  inodes         inodes in the table
+40  root           1, the root's inode number, written but not read back
+48  generation     reams of this volume so far
.EE
.PP
A volume is recognised by its magic and its block size; anything else
wrong in the superblock, or a
.I blocks
larger than the device holds, is refused rather than trusted.
.SS The bitmap
One bit per block of the whole volume, from block 0, set when the block
is taken.
A block's bit sits at the block's number divided by eight bits to the
byte, counting from
.IR bitmap_start .
The blocks that hold the superblock, the bitmap itself and the inode
table are marked taken when the volume is made, and never freed.
.SS The inode table
128 bytes per inode, thirty-two to a block, from
.IR inode_start .
Inode 0 is never handed out, so a directory entry naming inode 0 is
empty, and inode 1 is always the root.
.IP
.EX
+0   mode       0 for a free inode; otherwise the permission bits and,
               for a directory, bit 31 set
+4   version    counts every write; becomes the qid's version
+8   size       bytes, for a file; 4096 for a fresh directory
+16  mtime      always zero; nothing on this machine sets a file's time
+40  direct     twelve 4-byte block numbers, 0 for a hole
+88  indirect   a 4-byte block number of 1024 more, 0 until one is needed
.EE
.PP
The bytes from 24 to 39 and from 92 to 127 are zero and carry nothing.
Twelve direct blocks and one indirect block of 1024 more make a file of at
most 4243456 bytes; a block past that is refused.
.SS Data
Everything from
.IR data_start ,
handed out one block at a time by the bitmap.
A directory is a file whose blocks hold 128-byte entries:
.IP
.EX
+0   ino        a 4-byte inode number, 0 for an empty slot
+4   namelen    the name's length, one byte, at most 123
+5   name       exactly namelen bytes, not NUL-terminated
.EE
.PP
the rest of a used slot, and the whole of an empty one past
.IR ino ,
is zero.
A directory grows by one block, appended and zeroed, when its entries run
out.
A read of a file block the volume never wrote answers zeros, which is how
a truncate that grows, or a write past the old end, opens a hole.
.SS Order
There is no journal.
A block is marked taken in the bitmap before anything on the volume
points to it, a file's inode is written only after its blocks hold what
the inode's
.I size
will say, and a directory entry is written only after the inode it names
is whole.
A crash between two writes therefore loses at most the last operation and
leaves every earlier one whole.
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/kfs/disk.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR kfs (4),
.IR sd (3)
.SH BUGS
A block marked taken and never pointed at by an inode, which a crash
between an allocation and the write that would use it can leave, is
reclaimed only by reaming the volume.
